## 5. Writing the spec

- The spec is the only truth; there are no ADRs and no design notes that
  override it (AGENTS.md rule 1, RULED: CFG-1; `check-no-adr-citations`).
- A decision's design item may ADD spec text where its edit map is silent; it
  may never move or delete a sentence the map does not name (RULED: RS-21).
- Specs stay loosely coupled: a generated document carries no `§` numbers
  into another page's prose (the primer drops them from the directive
  registry for this reason; the owner's 1a of 2026-08-19,
  `ledger/rulings_2026_08_19_787_guide_and_packaging.md`).
- An effect is deny-by-default and named by the capability that grants it;
  an error is a value with a registered `CXER` code (`cxer-registry-gate`
  holds the registry to `spec/03-approved/process/governance.md`). Both are
  pinned by corpus cases in `contributor.md` §2. A surface that throws, or
  that reaches an effect with no capability, is a design defect before it is
  a code defect.
- A normative sentence has a corpus case beside it before any code moves:
  the case is how a later reader knows the sentence still holds.

## 6. The option table — what a structural decision shows

Every structural change is posed on its issue as the full option table, for
the owner's decision, before anything moves (RULED: OL-15); each option states
its long-term consequence for CX. The table covers every dimension at once:

| Dimension | What the option must say |
|---|---|
| code | which V module and directory; the imports it adds; the registration hook it uses |
| spec | which page, which section, added or changed sentences |
| conformance | which corpus file, which grader (`ring=`), which shard |
| gates | which step refuses the wrong tree, and whether one is added (`check-selection-manifest` then) |
| docs | which generated document moves (`make docs` in the same commit) |
| lockfile / pins | which `deps.cxd` row or `[module]` row changes |
| tooling | editors, completions, the migration tool (`cx --migrate-namespace`) |
